WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:11.640
- [Caroline] This is
the Warner Bros. Archive

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:13.020
in Los Angeles,

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.730
a prop warehouse that
helps preserve the history

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:18.410
of one of Hollywood's greatest studios.

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:22.470
And we had the rare
opportunity to take a tour.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:27.470
- We have everything from
cars, as you might see.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:29.960
We have props.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:34.240
We have over almost 30,000 costumes.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.350
- [Caroline] This is Bonnie Fallone Otto.

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:40.050
She's the director of the
Warner Bros. Corporate Archive.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:42.350
- And we have some set deck too.

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:45.960
- She showed us props and
costumes that go back decades

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.000
and explained how they maintain them

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.990
and why the archive is so important.

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:54.410
Oh, and I may have geeked
out just a little bit.

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:58.077
I just remembered that Leonardo
DiCaprio sat in this car.

00:00:58.077 --> 00:00:58.910
- Yes, he did!

00:00:59.109 --> 00:01:01.023
Oh, my gosh, does it smell like him?

00:01:02.718 --> 00:01:03.643
OK, oh, my God!

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.500
The first thing that strikes
you about the archive

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.723
is just how massive it is.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:22.003
And there was still a ton of
ground we didn't get to cover.

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:25.510
The "Batman" movies have
been a defining property

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:29.940
for Warner Bros. since Tim
Burton's iconic 1989 movie

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:32.477
starring Michael Keaton
and Jack Nicholson.

00:01:32.477 --> 00:01:34.537
- You can call me

00:01:34.537 --> 00:01:35.370
Joker.

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:38.240
- So from which movies
are these Batmobiles from?

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:40.700
- This one over here is
from "Batman Returns."

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.040
This is the Batmissile.

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:51.230
Over here, this is the spinner car

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:54.533
from Michael Keaton's
"Batman," so it's quite old.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:02.890
They used it for a sequence
where the Batmobile spun around.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:04.130
- Wow.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:06.650
- And then over here is the

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:11.980
George Clooney Batmobile
from "Batman and Robin."

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:14.823
So now if you want to sit in it.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:16.613
- I can?

00:02:16.613 --> 00:02:18.200
- Absolutely, go ahead, jump in.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:19.910
- I jump in?

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:20.743
Oh.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.480
How does George Clooney fit in here?

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:27.383
Oh, God.

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.780
I'm scared to move.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:34.030
And let's not forget this
giant tank-like Batmobile

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:37.030
from "Batman v Superman"
and "Justice League."

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:38.563
This is my Batman ride.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:44.910
It weighs 8,300 pounds and
is around 11.5 feet wide.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:48.660
It's not easy to move
it from place to place.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:50.050
Sometimes productions need

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:52.640
more than one version of the same car.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.380
This was Joker's car from "Suicide Squad."

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.630
One of these cars can actually drive,

00:02:57.630 --> 00:02:59.820
one was just used for static shots,

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:00.670
and the third one?

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:02.447
- That's ruining date night!

00:03:02.447 --> 00:03:04.140
Has a hole in its roof.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.310
But enough about "Batman" for a moment.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:09.470
There was another car I
was really excited about,

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:12.110
the Duesenberg from "The Great Gatsby."

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:13.540
- Custom job.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:14.799
Supercharged engine.

00:03:14.799 --> 00:03:15.632
- Ah.

00:03:15.632 --> 00:03:16.465
Wait, I just remembered that

00:03:16.465 --> 00:03:18.942
Leonardo DiCaprio sat in this car.

00:03:18.942 --> 00:03:19.775
- Yes, he did!

00:03:20.417 --> 00:03:25.120
Wow, Oh, my God, Leonardo
DiCaprio's butt was here.

00:03:25.969 --> 00:03:28.550
And look at all of these costumes.

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:31.020
They're stored in custom-made muslin bags

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:32.610
that help keep out moisture,

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:34.650
and it's a who's who in there.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:37.150
We saw some outfits
that were used in movies

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:41.240
as recent as 2018's "Crazy Rich Asians."

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:45.680
So this is the dress that was
worn in the engagement scene.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.210
And of course probably my favorite outfit

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:50.330
from all of the gowns and everything

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:54.790
was this that Awkwafina's
character wore inside her home.

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:57.970
And then we saw hats
worn in "My Fair Lady"

00:03:57.970 --> 00:03:59.239
dating all the way back to 1964.

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:03.773
♪ The rain in Spain stays
mainly in the plain ♪

00:04:03.773 --> 00:04:07.470
- [Bonnie] That was worn when she returned

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.453
to Covent Garden after she became a lady.

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:14.910
So the straw is in beautiful condition.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:15.750
- Yeah.

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:19.210
- [Bonnie] And even some of the colors

00:04:19.210 --> 00:04:21.090
are still pretty much intact.

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:22.730
- [Caroline] Especially delicate outfits

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:26.220
are wrapped in acid-free paper
and put in acid-free boxes

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:28.183
to keep them in mint condition.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:31.110
There was also a wide array

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:34.210
of other types of props and set pieces.

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:38.104
So Margot Robbie, um,
hello, and that's her bat.

00:05:02.421 --> 00:05:03.254
Ew!

00:05:05.461 --> 00:05:09.190
They actually have the hair on it too.

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:11.930
Preserving pieces of
film history like this

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:15.550
is incredibly important,
but it's not easy.

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:19.030
This archive has been around since 1992.

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:21.430
- Before the archive was set up,

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:23.100
there really was no protocol

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:25.540
as to where these assets were going,

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:29.073
so many times they went
home with cast and crew.

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:32.740
- [Caroline] A lot of
iconic movie props, puppets,

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:36.260
and animatronics are made
out of materials like foam,

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:39.340
which, sadly, deteriorate over the years.

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:40.800
- When you're in production,

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:42.930
all they care about is getting that shot.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:46.190
So a lot of times
they're not made to last,

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:49.910
and then they come here, and
we are commissioned to do that.

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:52.400
We've gotta figure out
the best way to make that,

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:54.850
at least preserve the life of that prop.

00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:57.600
- [Caroline] While the archive
isn't open to the public,

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.250
a lot of these props often go on tour,

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:04.163
so you too can one day get a
chance to sit in the Batmobile.

00:06:05.887 --> 00:06:07.129
Batman.

00:06:07.129 --> 00:06:08.263
No?

00:06:08.263 --> 00:06:09.275
Batman.

00:06:09.275 --> 00:06:10.942
It's my Batman ride.

